{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-225265",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-8{x}^{2}-30x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(4{x}^{2}+15x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $4{x}^{2}+15x-4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $4\\times (-4)=-16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $15$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-16$  are\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\,(-1,16),\\,(2,-8),\\,(-2,8),\\,(4,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,16)$ add to $15:$ \n\n$$\n-1+16=15\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-x+16x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+15x-4 & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x+16x-4 & = \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-x)+(16x-4) & = \\\\ x(4x-1)+4(4x-1) & = \\\\ (x+4)(4x-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(x+4)(4x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
